INT. FLOODED LIMESTONE CAVE - DAY

A low chamber, water to the waist. Headlamps stab the dark. Stalactites drip. Somewhere ahead, a pulse of water echoes.

Five climbers wade in a line. JOSEFINA LARRAZABAL (30s, expedition lead, braid) pushes ahead, rope coiled over one shoulder. TOMASZ EKWUEME (40s, doctor, glasses fogged) follows with a medical bag held above his head. MADELEINE PRICE-ODUYA (20s, geologist, hardhat) tests the wall with a hammer. RUBEN HAKIMI (30s, photographer, camera in a dry bag) stumbles. GUNNAR SIGURDSSON (50s, veteran caver, quiet, grey beard) brings up the rear, a hand on the rope, counting steps.

JOSEFINA
Water's rising.

GUNNAR
Not rising. Rain upstairs.

RUBEN
Is that better?

GUNNAR
It's the same.

Madeleine taps the wall. A hollow note answers. She tilts her head and taps again.

MADELEINE
It's thin here.

TOMASZ
How thin?

MADELEINE
Thin enough that he should not lean on it.

Ruben, who has been leaning, pulls back. The wall gives a long groan. Josefina swings around, grabs Ruben's collar, and drags him forward as a slab of limestone sheers off and drops into the water behind them. A wave slaps all five. Gunnar yanks Tomasz upright, Tomasz clutches his bag, Madeleine drops her hammer, and Josefina shoves Ruben against the far wall and shouts for them all to move.

JOSEFINA
Go! Go! Go!

They surge down the passage. The rope jerks tight. Gunnar, last in line, counts steps, and when he reaches thirty, he stops. The water behind them has gone still.

GUNNAR
That's the worst of it.

JOSEFINA
You're sure?

GUNNAR
No. But it sounds like it.

Tomasz lets out a laugh that is half a sob. Ruben clutches his camera. Madeleine fishes her hammer from the water and wipes it on her sleeve.

JOSEFINA
Everyone count off.

TOMASZ
One.

MADELEINE
Two.

RUBEN
Three.

GUNNAR
Four.

JOSEFINA
Five. Good. We keep moving.
